1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,310
Tonight on The Brief: a bargain perfume,

2
00:00:06,340 --> 00:00:10,250
a tag that wouldn't stay on, and the ruling that changed

3
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:16,090
how far a brand's reputation is protected. We begin with one shopper's story.

4
00:00:20,367 --> 00:00:23,757
Too strong, you are wasting your time.

5
00:00:26,527 --> 00:00:29,817
So this is La Valeur. Just like Trésor,

6
00:00:29,847 --> 00:00:32,477
it has the notes, the base notes of Trésor.

7
00:00:32,727 --> 00:00:33,577
Just try it once.

8
00:00:33,607 --> 00:00:34,807
Just try it.

9
00:00:36,687 --> 00:00:37,797
What happened?

10
00:00:37,827 --> 00:00:38,757
No, no, nothing.

11
00:00:38,787 --> 00:00:39,437
Nothing happened.

12
00:00:39,467 --> 00:00:40,077
You sure?

13
00:00:40,107 --> 00:00:41,677
No, everything is alright.

14
00:00:44,927 --> 00:00:49,697
Well, it smells like Trésor.

15
00:00:50,807 --> 00:00:52,007
It is like Trésor.

16
00:00:52,607 --> 00:00:54,397
Half the price and it is the same bottle.

17
00:00:54,587 --> 00:00:55,787
I am a little confused.

18
00:00:56,347 --> 00:00:56,877
It is like Trésor.

19
00:00:56,907 --> 00:00:59,077
As of now, it does smell like Trésor.

20
00:00:59,387 --> 00:01:00,587
Should I pack it up for you?

21
00:01:02,007 --> 00:01:03,077
It is half the price, right?

22
00:01:03,107 --> 00:01:04,217
Yeah, half the price.

23
00:01:04,247 --> 00:01:05,070
Okay, sure.

24
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:08,050
Wow, that smells amazing. Is it Trésor by L'Oréal?

25
00:01:08,540 --> 00:01:12,390
Oh, that? Actually, no. It's La Valeur by Bellure.

26
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:17,870
I would say it has the same smell, but it's half the price and it's similar.

27
00:01:17,967 --> 00:01:21,857
Our legal desk checked the case record.

28
00:01:22,167 --> 00:01:24,457
The answer in the real case was no.

29
00:01:24,567 --> 00:01:27,677
In L'Oréal v Bellure, the courts accepted that the

30
00:01:27,707 --> 00:01:29,517
bottles' similarity was not likely

31
00:01:29,547 --> 00:01:31,837
to mislead professionals or the public.

32
00:01:32,207 --> 00:01:34,657
The English Court of Appeal's findings go further.

33
00:01:35,147 --> 00:01:37,237
Customers and consumers were not deceived.

34
00:01:37,467 --> 00:01:41,457
There was no tarnishment or blurring of L'Oréal's marks,

35
00:01:41,747 --> 00:01:43,817
and L'Oréal's own sales were not

36
00:01:43,847 --> 00:01:44,537
affected.

37
00:01:44,567 --> 00:01:48,757
So, if nobody was deceived, why did this

38
00:01:48,787 --> 00:01:51,257
reach Europe's highest court?

39
00:01:51,367 --> 00:01:53,517
Because the legal wrong wasn't deception,

40
00:01:53,847 --> 00:01:56,777
it was free-riding: using Trésor's name and

41
00:01:56,807 --> 00:02:00,637
prestige to sell a rival product without paying for the reputation.

42
00:02:00,847 --> 00:02:03,337
But there's an important distinction here.

43
00:02:03,407 --> 00:02:05,777
Taking inspiration from a famous brand is

44
00:02:05,807 --> 00:02:09,497
not automatically unlawful. The real question is whether

45
00:02:09,527 --> 00:02:11,477
the competitor is merely offering

46
00:02:11,507 --> 00:02:14,717
consumers an alternative, or exploiting the reputation

47
00:02:14,747 --> 00:02:16,297
built by the original brand to

48
00:02:16,327 --> 00:02:18,977
gain an unfair commercial advantage.

49
00:02:19,067 --> 00:02:22,037
That distinction is what makes this case so significant

50
00:02:22,067 --> 00:02:24,197
for the law of comparative

51
00:02:24,967 --> 00:02:27,357
advertising and trade mark protection.

52
00:02:27,467 --> 00:02:29,897
The claimants were L'Oréal and Lancôme Parfums.

53
00:02:29,927 --> 00:02:32,457
They hold UK registrations for perfumes, for the

54
00:02:32,487 --> 00:02:35,777
word marks Trésor, Miracle, Anaïs-Anaïs and Noa,

55
00:02:35,807 --> 00:02:38,177
and for the bottle and packaging of Trésor

56
00:02:38,207 --> 00:02:41,337
and Miracle. The defendants were Bellure NV,

57
00:02:41,667 --> 00:02:43,457
which produced the Creation Lamis and

58
00:02:43,487 --> 00:02:47,057
Dorall ranges, with Starion International and Malaika

59
00:02:47,087 --> 00:02:49,657
Investments distributing them. La Valeur

60
00:02:49,687 --> 00:02:52,977
imitated Trésor, and Pink Wonder imitated Miracle,

61
00:02:53,007 --> 00:02:55,477
in bottles and boxes generally similar

62
00:02:55,507 --> 00:02:58,657
to the originals. Two things were challenged.

63
00:02:58,687 --> 00:03:01,637
First, Starion and Malaika's comparison

64
00:03:01,667 --> 00:03:04,917
lists, given to retailers, matching each perfume

65
00:03:04,947 --> 00:03:07,437
to the famous one by its registered

66
00:03:07,467 --> 00:03:10,057
name. Second, the bottles and packaging,

67
00:03:10,087 --> 00:03:13,177
under the UK's version of the dilution provision,

68
00:03:13,207 --> 00:03:16,397
section 10(3) of the Trade Marks Act 1994.

69
00:03:16,427 --> 00:03:20,097
The Advocate General records that these

70
00:03:20,127 --> 00:03:23,317
perfumes retailed for under £4.

71
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:25,710
Five important legal questions were referred,

72
00:03:25,740 --> 00:03:27,970
and they boiled down to three issues.

73
00:03:28,240 --> 00:03:30,610
The first issue: dilution and free-riding.

74
00:03:30,860 --> 00:03:33,170
Under Article 5(2) of Directive 89/104,

75
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:38,650
can a third party take unfair advantage

76
00:03:38,680 --> 00:03:43,290
of a famous mark even with no confusion and no harm to the mark?

77
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:45,070
The court answered this question first,

78
00:03:45,100 --> 00:03:49,730
because Article 5(2) could also apply to

79
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:51,070
the comparison lists.

80
00:03:51,440 --> 00:03:53,730
The second issue: mark functions.

81
00:03:53,780 --> 00:03:59,650
Under Article 5(1)(a): identical mark, identical goods.

82
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:03,250
Can the owner stop comparison-list use even when

83
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:05,790
the origin function is untouched?

84
00:04:06,280 --> 00:04:08,910
And issue three: comparative advertising.

85
00:04:09,140 --> 00:04:13,710
Under Article 3a(1)(g) and (h) of Directive

86
00:04:13,740 --> 00:04:17,510
84/450, does a truthful

87
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:20,890
"smells like" list take unfair advantage,

88
00:04:20,920 --> 00:04:23,970
or present goods as an imitation or replica?

89
00:04:24,067 --> 00:04:28,737
Two analysts argue the opposing positions as they were put to the court.

90
00:04:29,207 --> 00:04:33,397
So the first one we have is Anushka, who is representing L'Oréal,

91
00:04:33,427 --> 00:04:38,297
and the other counsel we have is Athar, who is representing Bellure.

92
00:04:39,867 --> 00:04:44,057
L'Oréal's case is that Bellure takes a free ride. Trésor's image took years

93
00:04:44,087 --> 00:04:45,497
and heavy investment to build.

94
00:04:45,527 --> 00:04:47,817
The lists name our registered marks so that a four-pound

95
00:04:47,847 --> 00:04:49,597
smell-alike borrows our image.

96
00:04:50,547 --> 00:04:52,717
Article 5(2) protects a mark with a reputation against

97
00:04:52,747 --> 00:04:55,957
unfair advantage and undue exploitation.

98
00:04:56,267 --> 00:04:58,857
Nothing in its wording requires confusion or detriment.

99
00:04:59,567 --> 00:05:01,537
The referring court found a link in the public's mind,

100
00:05:01,567 --> 00:05:04,457
a commercial advantage for the defendants, and an intentional similarity.

101
00:05:04,807 --> 00:05:07,197
Our second point is Article 5(1)(a),

102
00:05:07,607 --> 00:05:11,057
according to which identical marks on identical goods are infringed

103
00:05:11,087 --> 00:05:13,557
where the use affects a function of the mark,

104
00:05:13,587 --> 00:05:17,237
and a mark's functions include communication, investment and advertising,

105
00:05:17,667 --> 00:05:18,757
not just origin.

106
00:05:18,787 --> 00:05:20,777
Third, the comparative advertising directive only

107
00:05:20,807 --> 00:05:22,677
permits use of a rival's mark

108
00:05:22,707 --> 00:05:24,297
if every condition is met.

109
00:05:24,387 --> 00:05:26,497
Their lists present the goods as imitations or

110
00:05:26,527 --> 00:05:28,377
replicas, and that fails the test.

111
00:05:28,467 --> 00:05:32,437
Our case is that nothing here was untrue.

112
00:05:32,467 --> 00:05:36,277
A smell-alike is lawful to make, and the only practical

113
00:05:36,307 --> 00:05:41,197
way to describe a smell is to say it

114
00:05:41,227 --> 00:05:43,597
smells like another. Buyers

115
00:05:43,627 --> 00:05:48,917
know exactly what they are getting. So the origin function is untouched,

116
00:05:49,177 --> 00:05:51,497
and that is the settled limit on Article 5(1)(a).

117
00:05:51,527 --> 00:05:56,997
Second, unfair has to mean something. There was

118
00:05:57,027 --> 00:05:59,897
no blurring, no tarnishment and no lost sales.

119
00:06:00,547 --> 00:06:03,137
Where is the injury? A benefit alone is just

120
00:06:03,167 --> 00:06:05,757
competition, and English law has no tort of unfair

121
00:06:05,787 --> 00:06:07,997
competition. L'Oréal is asking the

122
00:06:08,027 --> 00:06:12,597
court to create one through trade mark law.

123
00:06:12,827 --> 00:06:14,597
Third, the legislature said comparative

124
00:06:14,627 --> 00:06:18,237
advertising should be encouraged. If naming a rival's

125
00:06:18,267 --> 00:06:21,597
product to compare is infringement,

126
00:06:21,627 --> 00:06:26,837
then generic medicines, printer cartridges and spare parts are all at risk.

127
00:06:27,327 --> 00:06:31,497
By effectively banning reference to benchmark signs,

128
00:06:31,527 --> 00:06:34,017
the ruling stifles legitimate

129
00:06:34,047 --> 00:06:36,557
low-cost competition and denies budget-conscious consumers

130
00:06:36,587 --> 00:06:39,577
access to affordable alternatives

131
00:06:39,607 --> 00:06:42,817
priced at just a fraction of luxury brands.

132
00:06:42,847 --> 00:06:45,217
Consumers were fully aware they were

133
00:06:45,247 --> 00:06:48,977
buying an inexpensive smell-alike, not genuine L'Oréal.

134
00:06:49,007 --> 00:06:52,157
Origin confusion was zero, and L'Oréal

135
00:06:52,187 --> 00:06:54,677
suffered no demonstrable financial

136
00:06:54,707 --> 00:06:58,717
loss or sales diversion.

137
00:06:58,800 --> 00:07:02,090
The directive's conditions are cumulative. You may use our mark to compare

138
00:07:02,120 --> 00:07:05,290
only if you don't take unfair advantage and don't present your goods as an

139
00:07:05,320 --> 00:07:07,790
imitation or replica. Your lists do both.

140
00:07:08,360 --> 00:07:12,530
Infringement without confusion under Article 5(2), and equivalent dilution

141
00:07:12,560 --> 00:07:17,370
frameworks like India's Section 29(4), proves consumer confusion or actual

142
00:07:17,400 --> 00:07:20,790
commercial damage is not required once unfair advantage and intentional association

143
00:07:20,820 --> 00:07:22,290
have been established.

144
00:07:22,320 --> 00:07:28,250
My smell is not a trade mark. The Advocate General agreed with us on the lists.

145
00:07:28,280 --> 00:07:32,390
He said Article 5(1)(a) should not stop comparative use unless it affects a function of

146
00:07:32,420 --> 00:07:36,010
the mark, and that comparison alone is not an unfair advantage.

147
00:07:36,100 --> 00:07:40,250
The court sided with L'Oréal on three points.

148
00:07:40,600 --> 00:07:44,750
One: unfair advantage under Article 5(2)

149
00:07:44,780 --> 00:07:47,990
needs no confusion and no detriment to the mark.

150
00:07:48,060 --> 00:07:52,350
It is enough that the third party rides on the coat-tails of the mark

151
00:07:52,380 --> 00:07:56,130
to exploit, without paying, the owner's marketing effort.

152
00:07:56,440 --> 00:08:00,030
The test is a global assessment: the strength of the reputation,

153
00:08:00,300 --> 00:08:02,250
the similarity, and the closeness of the goods.

154
00:08:02,280 --> 00:08:06,350
Two: under Article 5(1)(a),

155
00:08:06,520 --> 00:08:10,830
the owner can stop use of an identical sign for identical goods

156
00:08:10,860 --> 00:08:14,150
in comparative advertising that fails the conditions,

157
00:08:14,180 --> 00:08:17,130
even where the origin function is untouched,

158
00:08:17,640 --> 00:08:19,890
if another function, such as communication,

159
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:22,750
investment or advertising, is affected.

160
00:08:23,100 --> 00:08:25,890
Three: saying, explicitly or implicitly,

161
00:08:25,920 --> 00:08:31,450
that your product is an imitation presents it as an imitation or replica,

162
00:08:31,480 --> 00:08:34,630
even if only the smell is imitated.

163
00:08:34,940 --> 00:08:38,450
Any advantage gained that way is unfair.

164
00:08:38,567 --> 00:08:41,977
Let's start with the critical analysis now.

165
00:08:42,107 --> 00:08:46,017
So the law is clear. Whether it is right is the live question.

166
00:08:46,067 --> 00:08:50,477
Our desk condenses the debate into three balanced critical lenses.

167
00:08:51,007 --> 00:08:53,017
First, the doctrinal critique.

168
00:08:53,667 --> 00:08:56,777
Traditional trade mark law protects consumers from confusion.

169
00:08:56,947 --> 00:09:01,477
Here, nobody was confused, no sales were diverted and L'Oréal lost nothing.

170
00:09:01,727 --> 00:09:04,737
The court shifted the rationale from consumer protection

171
00:09:04,767 --> 00:09:06,797
to protecting the brand owner's

172
00:09:06,827 --> 00:09:10,497
investment, treating reputation almost like property.

173
00:09:10,727 --> 00:09:13,657
Lord Justice Jacob, who made the reference,

174
00:09:13,687 --> 00:09:17,797
said this reads the word "unfair" out of "unfair advantage",

175
00:09:17,827 --> 00:09:21,417
so that all free-riding is condemned per se.

176
00:09:21,807 --> 00:09:24,537
And look at the reasoning: the advantage is unfair

177
00:09:24,567 --> 00:09:26,777
because the advertising is unlawful,

178
00:09:26,807 --> 00:09:31,357
and the advertising is unlawful because it takes an unfair advantage.

179
00:09:31,447 --> 00:09:33,117
That is completely circular.

180
00:09:33,147 --> 00:09:38,057
The court never said what makes a benefit unfair beyond

181
00:09:38,087 --> 00:09:40,697
the fact that the owner did not get paid.

182
00:09:41,227 --> 00:09:45,377
Academic critics, including Gangjee and Burrell, made the same point.

183
00:09:45,607 --> 00:09:49,377
Protection expanded without a convincing justification, and even the

184
00:09:49,407 --> 00:09:53,637
Advocate General would have drawn the line far more narrowly.

185
00:09:54,107 --> 00:09:58,097
An unfair advantage should be proved with concrete evidence,

186
00:09:58,187 --> 00:10:00,177
not presumed from prestige alone.

187
00:10:00,207 --> 00:10:03,557
The first lesson: a trade mark was never meant to

188
00:10:03,587 --> 00:10:06,677
guard a brand against being admired.

189
00:10:06,767 --> 00:10:08,017
Let's state the case for the court.

190
00:10:08,047 --> 00:10:09,137
Defend the ruling honestly.

191
00:10:09,167 --> 00:10:11,617
If copying prestige is free, who funds the prestige?

192
00:10:11,907 --> 00:10:13,737
A brand is built by years of advertising,

193
00:10:13,767 --> 00:10:15,757
master perfumers and expensive packaging.

194
00:10:15,927 --> 00:10:17,717
The defendant bought none of that.

195
00:10:17,887 --> 00:10:21,117
And the evidence showed it deliberately winked at the originals.

196
00:10:21,147 --> 00:10:24,377
The trial judge explicitly found that the similarity was intentional.

197
00:10:24,547 --> 00:10:26,937
The harm may also be quieter than a loss of sales.

198
00:10:27,047 --> 00:10:29,517
A brand that is everywhere in cheap imitation loses

199
00:10:29,547 --> 00:10:31,617
its exclusivity, which is exactly what

200
00:10:31,647 --> 00:10:32,777
luxury sells.

201
00:10:32,807 --> 00:10:36,717
Intent is not required under the directive, but here it was found.

202
00:10:36,947 --> 00:10:40,957
There is a crucial fairness point that the critics skip.

203
00:10:41,007 --> 00:10:43,277
The ruling does not stop anyone from selling cheap

204
00:10:43,307 --> 00:10:45,017
perfume or copying the scent.

205
00:10:45,087 --> 00:10:47,977
It only stops them from trading on a registered name to do it.

206
00:10:48,147 --> 00:10:49,917
Bellure could have sold the same smell under its

207
00:10:49,947 --> 00:10:52,157
own name, but it did not do that.

208
00:10:52,267 --> 00:10:54,437
L'Oréal's name was used as a shortcut.

209
00:10:55,107 --> 00:10:57,997
Protecting that massive investment against an unearned

210
00:10:58,027 --> 00:10:59,197
ride is a modest restraint

211
00:10:59,227 --> 00:11:00,157
for a legitimate benefit.

212
00:11:00,187 --> 00:11:03,537
The only weak spot is that the court refused to ask

213
00:11:03,567 --> 00:11:04,837
whether real economic harm followed,

214
00:11:04,867 --> 00:11:07,877
because even a fair principle needs an objective limit.

215
00:11:07,967 --> 00:11:09,737
...the market and the information lens.

216
00:11:09,787 --> 00:11:13,137
Markets rely on comparison, because comparison is how consumers know.

217
00:11:13,287 --> 00:11:16,557
A scent profile defies description without an established benchmark,

218
00:11:16,587 --> 00:11:19,537
and truthful, price-based comparison is precisely what the Comparative

219
00:11:19,567 --> 00:11:22,297
Advertising Directive set out to encourage.

220
00:11:22,487 --> 00:11:25,657
Yet under Bellure, even an entirely accurate smell-alike

221
00:11:25,687 --> 00:11:29,237
list is deemed unlawful. Truthfulness offers no defence.

222
00:11:29,887 --> 00:11:32,057
Stretch that logic across other sectors,

223
00:11:32,087 --> 00:11:36,197
and the generic medicine, the compatible cartridge or the spare part

224
00:11:36,227 --> 00:11:39,417
faces immediate legal risk the moment it claims

225
00:11:39,447 --> 00:11:41,257
equivalence to a market leader.

226
00:11:41,527 --> 00:11:43,957
This directly distorts downstream competition.

227
00:11:44,207 --> 00:11:47,177
Budget alternatives compete on one thing: price,

228
00:11:47,367 --> 00:11:51,117
the oldest and most fundamental driver of consumer welfare,

229
00:11:51,147 --> 00:11:55,757
and they depend entirely on buyers recognising that functional equivalence.

230
00:11:55,947 --> 00:11:59,517
By treating that mental association as an unlawful commercial taking,

231
00:11:59,847 --> 00:12:02,157
the ruling shelters deep-pocketed incumbents,

232
00:12:02,187 --> 00:12:05,177
penalises supermarket own labels and leaves price-conscious

233
00:12:05,207 --> 00:12:06,677
consumers in the dark.

234
00:12:06,727 --> 00:12:09,257
The court itself soon recognised this overreach.

235
00:12:09,307 --> 00:12:12,577
In Interflora v Marks & Spencer, the Court of Justice quietly retreated

236
00:12:12,607 --> 00:12:14,697
from the absolute breadth of Bellure,

237
00:12:14,727 --> 00:12:17,417
conceding that advertising a genuine market alternative

238
00:12:17,447 --> 00:12:19,097
is not inherently unfair.

239
00:12:19,127 --> 00:12:21,057
In the end, the court targeted the right harm,

240
00:12:21,547 --> 00:12:25,297
preventing reputed marks from being co-opted as direct

241
00:12:25,367 --> 00:12:28,377
selling props, but framed the legal principle far too broadly.

242
00:12:28,407 --> 00:12:31,857
A balanced doctrine would protect investment without

243
00:12:31,887 --> 00:12:34,517
stifling legitimate trade,

244
00:12:34,547 --> 00:12:38,377
anchoring unfair advantage to standards of honest commercial practice

245
00:12:38,407 --> 00:12:41,337
and tangible economic harm, so truthful competition survives

246
00:12:41,367 --> 00:12:43,337
while pure exploitation remains wrong.

247
00:12:43,433 --> 00:12:47,183
This issue is not just history. It is relevant to TikTok,

248
00:12:47,513 --> 00:12:48,603
influencers and quick commerce

249
00:12:48,633 --> 00:12:49,423
today.

250
00:12:49,453 --> 00:12:53,683
Sellers openly market products as dupes or "smells like" perfumes.

251
00:12:53,893 --> 00:12:56,763
In Lidl v Tesco, the English Court of Appeal upheld

252
00:12:56,793 --> 00:12:59,303
the finding that Tesco's prices took

253
00:12:59,333 --> 00:13:02,723
unfair advantage of Lidl's reputation for low prices.

254
00:13:02,853 --> 00:13:04,283
Confusion was not required.

255
